preConditionHook:
// THE LEVEL TEST THAT SEPARATES A BREAKOUT PULLBACK FROM A FAILED BREAKOUT.
// After a bull breakout, the pullback trades back BEYOND the breakout point
// -- the low goes under the level the breakout bar closed above -- and the
// read flips: this is no longer a pullback to buy, it is a failed breakout.
// The source states it as exactly this negation gate. Short is the mirror.
//
// The breakout bar is found by the same bounded backward scan
// BreakoutPullbackWindow and BreakoutPullbackEntry use: the most recent bar k
// in 1..startWindow whose close is beyond the extreme of the breakoutLookback
// bars ending one bar before it. The level is open-coded per candidate
// because Highest(h, N)[k + 1] is a function-history reference at a
// VARIABLE offset; Extremes(), which Highest and Lowest call, is EL source
// and is exactly this loop, so the comparisons carry the EL relational
// tolerance. 'Most recent' is carried by the brkUpBar == -1 guard rather than
// a loop break, because EasyLanguage has none and both twins must run the
// same iterations.
//
// lookback EXTENDS THE LEVEL BACK INTO THE SPIKE. At the default 1 the level
// is the breakout point itself, which is what the rest of the bucket
// remembers. At 2 it also takes in the breakout bar's own low, at 3 the bar
// before that -- the second source's deeper reading, 'back beyond where the
// spike began', as one parameter over the spike's bars rather than a second
// rule. The extension sits inside an IF on the -1 guard: EasyLanguage does
// not short-circuit, and low[-1] is a future bar.
//
// Not BreakoutLevelRetest, which wants the pullback to HOLD the level (a
// touch within a tolerance, a capped overshoot, and a swing pivot rather than
// a Donchian level); this rule is the outright violation, and the two are not
// negations of each other because 'never came back at all' fails both. Not
// BreakoutPullbackWindow either -- that one says a pullback is in progress
// and says nothing about the level.
//
// startWindow and breakoutLookback are PROMOTED at BreakoutPullbackWindow's
// defaults: the merged pseudocode leaves 'the breakout' implicit and the two
// rules are meant to be tuned together. The deepest bar touched is
// startWindow + breakoutLookback.
brkUpBar = -1;
brkDnBar = -1;
brkUpLvl = 0.0;
brkDnLvl = 0.0;
for (int k = 1; k <= (int)startWindow; ++k) {
    double lvlHigh = high[k + 1];
    double lvlLow = low[k + 1];
    for (int j = k + 2; j <= k + (int)breakoutLookback; ++j) {
        if (el_gt(high[j], lvlHigh)) lvlHigh = high[j];
        if (el_lt(low[j], lvlLow)) lvlLow = low[j];
    }
    if (brkUpBar == -1 && el_gt(close[k], lvlHigh)) {
        brkUpBar = k;
        brkUpLvl = lvlHigh;
    }
    if (brkDnBar == -1 && el_lt(close[k], lvlLow)) {
        brkDnBar = k;
        brkDnLvl = lvlLow;
    }
}
if (brkUpBar != -1) {
    for (int j = 1; j <= (int)lookback - 1; ++j) {
        if (el_lt(low[brkUpBar + j - 1], brkUpLvl)) brkUpLvl = low[brkUpBar + j - 1];
    }
}
if (brkDnBar != -1) {
    for (int j = 1; j <= (int)lookback - 1; ++j) {
        if (el_gt(high[brkDnBar + j - 1], brkDnLvl)) brkDnLvl = high[brkDnBar + j - 1];
    }
}
beyondUp = false;
if (brkUpBar != -1) {
    beyondUp = el_lt(low[0], brkUpLvl);
}
beyondDn = false;
if (brkDnBar != -1) {
    beyondDn = el_gt(high[0], brkDnLvl);
}
